-- title:
--   Uniqueness of the greedy scan trace
-- statement:
--   Write $I_n=\{0,\ldots,n-1\}$, with cyclic arithmetic modulo $n$ and integer representatives for inequalities; $[x]_n$ denotes the least nonnegative residue when $n>0$. Words use a forward cyclic rotation $L$ and the exchange $X=(0\ 1)$; $\operatorname{ev}(w)$ is their permutation product and $|w|$ their number of letters. A greedy scan compares the first two integer targets, exchanges a strict descent, and otherwise advances the cyclic cursor, stopping at the zero board. For its trace $g$, write $P_c(g)=\#_L(g)+((c-\#_L(g))\bmod n)$ for the paid rotation count. Let $n>0$, let $d:I_n\to\mathbb Z$, and suppose $u$ and $v$ are greedy scan traces from $d$. Then
--
--   $$
--   u=v.
--   $$
--
--   The trace relation determines one literal word whenever a terminating trace exists.
